Based on \eqref{eq:38e}, the mean and variance of $q( {{{\boldsymbol{\mathsf{G}}}_{t - 1}}| {{{\boldsymbol{\mathsf{G}}}_t},{{\boldsymbol{\mathsf{G}}}_0}} } )$ are explicitly expressed as:
\begin{align}
	\label{eq:41}
	{{{{\tilde {\boldsymbol{\mu}}_t} }}}\left( {{{\boldsymbol{\mathsf{G}}}_t},{{\boldsymbol{\mathsf{G}}}_0}} \right) &= \frac{1}{{\sqrt {{\alpha _t}} }}\left({{{\boldsymbol{\mathsf{G}}}}_t} - \frac{{{\beta _t}}}{{\sqrt {1 - {{\bar \alpha }_t}} }}{\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}_t}\right),\\
	\label{eq:}
	{{\tilde \beta }_t} &= \frac{{1 - {{\bar \alpha }_{t - 1}}}}{{1 - {{\bar \alpha }_t}}}{\beta _t}.
\end{align}
Generally, the variance $\mathbf{\Sigma}_{{\boldsymbol{\theta}}} {({ \boldsymbol{\dot{\mathsf{G}}}}, {{{\boldsymbol{\mathsf{G}}}_t},t} )}$ is set as a constant ${{\tilde \beta }_t}{\mathbf{I}}$ \cite{Ho11,jinglobecom}. Therefore, to ensure that the denoising transition density closely approximates the ground-truth denoising transition density, we can simplify the optimization of the KL divergence term to minimizing the difference between the expectations of the above two distributions. In this case, we only need to train CGDM to predict ${{{{\tilde {\boldsymbol{\mu}}_t} }}}( {{{\boldsymbol{\mathsf{G}}}_t},{{\boldsymbol{\mathsf{G}}}_0}})$:
\begin{align}\label{eq:42}
	{{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_{\boldsymbol{\theta }}}\left( {{ \boldsymbol{\dot{\mathsf{G}}}}, {{\boldsymbol{\mathsf{G}}}_t},t} \right) \!=\! \frac{1}{{\sqrt {{\alpha _t}} }}\left( {{{\boldsymbol{\mathsf{G}}}_t} - \frac{{1 - {\alpha _t}}}{{\sqrt {1 - {{\bar \alpha }_t}} }}{{\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}}_{\boldsymbol{\theta }}}\left( {{ \boldsymbol{\dot{\mathsf{G}}}}, {{\boldsymbol{\mathsf{G}}}_t},t} \right)} \right),
\end{align}
%\begin{align}\label{eq:}
