\section{Related Work}
% \subsection{Conjunctive Searchable Symmetric Encryption (CSSE)}
\noindent \textbf{Conjunctive Searchable Symmetric Encryption (CSSE). }
CSSE enables secure multi-keyword document retrieval, with early methods suffering from inefficiency or leakage. Golle et al.~\cite{acns04-golle} laid foundational work, inspiring advancements in boolean queries~\cite{crypto13-oxt,sp14-blindseer,tdsc22-Ferreira}, dynamic updates~\cite{icde17-ibtree, vldb19-vbtree, ndss21-odxt}, and fuzzy search~\cite{tifs16-fuzzyfu}. Modern implementations of CSSE~\cite{crypto13-oxt, ccs18-hxt, ndss21-odxt, ccs24-doris} predominantly build upon the OXT~\cite{crypto13-oxt} framework, with first retrieving documents via the least frequent keyword (\texttt{s-term}) to minimize search complexity, then filtering for full conjunctions. This wide-used construction reduces leakage but still reveals structural information, inspiring attacks exploiting such leaks.

% Conjunctive Searchable Symmetric Encryption (CSSE) addresses the challenge of securely retrieving documents matching multiple keywords. Early naive methods, like intersecting single-keyword results or computing meta-keywords, are inefficient and leaky. Golle et al.~\cite{acns04-golle} pioneered secure conjunctive search, prompting subsequent research to extend its capabilities to boolean queries~\cite{crypto13-oxt, sp14-blindseer, tdsc22-Ferreira}, dynamic update~\cite{icde17-ibtree, vldb19-vbtree, ndss21-odxt, tdsc24-guo}, and specialized scenarios like fuzzy search~\cite{tifs16-fuzzyfu}. 
% Recently, a two-stage query architecture widely adopted in efficient schemes. The first stage is a single-keyword query process to retrieve documents that match the least frequent term in conjunctive queries, designed to minimize search time complexity. Then, the server returns documents that match all keywords in each query. Many schemes~\cite{crypto13-oxt, ccs18-hxt, ndss21-odxt, ccs24-doris} utilize {\ttfamily TSet} construction, which leaks the volume pattern (the number of documents containing the single keyword) rather than the access pattern during the first stage.

% Cash et al.~\cite{crypto13-oxt} introduced a scheme utilizing {\ttfamily TSet} construction, which leaks the volume pattern (the number of documents matching the single keyword) rather than the access pattern during the first stage. Lai et al.~\cite{ccs18-hxt} proposed an enhanced HXT protocol to eliminate the KPRP leakage in~\cite{crypto13-oxt}. Numerous subsequent schemes have adopted {\ttfamily TSet} data structure~\cite{ccs24-doris, ndss21-odxt}, making this two-stage structure currently one of the most secure in terms of minimizing leakage in the first stage. However, in the second stage, nearly all schemes reveal the entire access pattern (often referred to as the whole result pattern, WRP) and the search pattern.

\noindent \textbf{Leakage-abuse attacks (LAAs). }
LAAs have emerged as a critically concerning threat, targeting the security vulnerabilities of Searchable Symmetric Encryption (SSE) systems during their real-world deployment. The first pioneering work in this area was introduced by Islam et al.~\cite{ndss12-ikk} and later improved by Cash et al.~\cite{ccs15-count}. Since then, a substantial body of related research has emerged under different adversarial models. 
Recently, a wide range of LAAs target the single-keyword search scenario. These include active attacks~\cite{usenix16-all, ndss20-revisiting, usenix23-injection}, which attempt to influence the system by injecting specific files to manipulate the search process, and passive attacks which rely on statistical analysis and correlations between leaked information and background knowledge in the form of known datasets~\cite{ndss12-ikk, ccs15-count, ndss20-revisiting, ccs21-leap, ccs23-dynamic, tifs19-ning, tifs21-xulei} or similar datasets~\cite{is14-freq, usenix21-sap, usenix21-refscore, usenix22-ihop, sp23-rethinking, ccs23-dynamic, usenix24-jigsaw} to compromise query privacy. 
% Among them, Oya et al.~\cite{usenix22-ihop} propose IHOP, modeling query recovery as a quadratic optimization problem solved via linear assignment iteration, showing statistical query recovery attacks threaten privacy-preserving SSE. Nie et al.~\cite{usenix24-jigsaw} reveal keywords with higher volume and query frequency are easier to recover, proposing to first recover keywords with high volume and query frequency and these results for subsequent query recovery. Xu et al.~\cite{ccs23-dynamic} extend LAAs to dynamic schemes, reconstructing search patterns via query frequency or volume information to break forward and backward privacy. 
Although LAAs have been extensively researched and explored in the single-keyword search scenario, a major limitation remains: single-keyword search is not practical for real-world applications. 

Zhang et al.~\cite{usenix16-all} extended their attack to the conjunctive query scenario, however, their approach relies on active file injection, which is infeasible in most real-world scenarios and incompatible with forward-secure schemes. Dijkslag et al.~\cite{c22-ckws} first explored the passive query recovery attack against secure conjunctive keyword search schemes. They proposed an easy and generic extension strategy that adapts query-recovery attacks from single-keyword searches by simply substituting the single-keyword set with a keyword conjunction set. Unfortunately, their experimental results show that the attack performs poorly on similar datasets with huge time and space overhead, even if they have access to a set of known queries as part of the attacker's knowledge. 
% In contrast, our work fully exploits the unique leakage patterns in conjunctive query scenarios, transforming the otherwise intractable combinatorial explosion of keyword conjunctions into a tractable problem and achieving substantial attack effectiveness without requiring known queries.

\noindent \textbf{Leakage suppression. }
To mitigate leakage-abuse attacks (LAAs), various defenses~\cite{ndss12-ikk, ccs15-count, infocom18-clrz, usenix20-seal} have been proposed. Obfuscation~\cite{infocom18-clrz} is a widely adopted approach, where a document matching a queried keyword is returned with probability $p$ (the true positive rate, TPR), while a non-matching document is returned with probability $q$ (the false positive rate, FPR), thereby introducing uncertainty in query outcomes. Another strategy involves volume padding. Cash et al.~\cite{ccs15-count} introduced a foundational padding technique that adjusts the volume to align with the closest multiple of a predefined integer $k$. Demertzis et al.~\cite{usenix20-seal} developed SEAL, which further modifies the response size to the nearest power of an integer $x$.


