% Balancing privacy and utility in synthetic data is particularly challenging in sensitive fields like healthcare. Privacy-preserving methods such as differential privacy provide strong theoretical guarantees but often do so at the cost of data quality and realism, which are crucial for healthcare applications. Clinical research, for example, requires data with high fidelity to gain meaningful insights, such as predicting health risks, identifying treatment patterns, and analyzing health trends. However, strict privacy measures can compromise the quality of synthetic data needed for these analyses.

% In machine learning, training models on synthetic data with preserved statistical properties is essential. However, many privacy techniques—particularly those involving noise addition—can distort these properties, leading to less effective or biased models. This is a significant concern when the goal is to build models that generalize well to real-world healthcare scenarios.

% \section{Ethical Considerations and Longitudinal Data Challenges}
