\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}




\IEEEPARstart{T}{he} proliferation of Internet of Things (IoT) technologies introduces security challenges that traditional methods often cannot handle effectively \cite{iot_6g_martalo2024cross}. Resource-constrained devices generate huge amounts of data; relying on centralized servers to process those data may lead to transfer delays, increased network load, and additional power consumption \cite{moura2024edge_iot_power_2}. Ideally, dataflow monitoring should be carried out on edge devices to limit overhead in network management \cite{shahin2024advancing_iot_dataflow}. This paper proposes a design strategy to enable efficient traffic classification (TC) on constrained devices.


The exponential growth of encrypted Internet traffic, which accounts for up to 90\% of data transmission \cite{enisa2023encrypted}, seems to worsen this scenario. Widespread encryption complicates network security and traffic analysis \cite{seydali2023cbs, lotfollahi2020deep}; modern protocols conceal packet contents, thus making traditional methods, such as Deep Packet Inspection (DPI) and port-based classification, mostly ineffective\cite{survey_DONG2024128444}.


Efficient real-time traffic analysis is essential in the cybersecurity ecosystem of today, especially when dealing with resource-constrained IoT and edge platforms \cite{habiba2023edge-ecosystem}. These devices require low-latency adaptive processing to manage encrypted traffic patterns and support real-time decision-making. Practical environments range from high-bandwidth 5G applications (such as video streaming and gaming that require uninterrupted performance \cite{adil20245g}), to sensitive sectors such as finance and healthcare, where secure communications are of paramount importance\cite{jan2021security}.

Deep neural networks (DNNs) are increasingly being used for TC, thanks to their accuracy performances and their ability to embed the feature-extraction process \cite{nas_etc_malekghaini2023automl4etc, seydali2023cbs}. At the same time, considerable computational requirements limit their use in resource-constrained environments \cite{tabanelli2023dnn}. This work explores strategies to optimize DNN architectures specifically for these constrained settings.



In addition to the DNN architecture itself, the optimization of classification performance requires careful choices of additional design aspects. First, network traffic can be classified at different levels of granularity, namely packets, flows, and sessions \cite{survey_wang2022machine}. A packet is the smallest data transmission unit, whereas a flow includes the packets sent from a source to some destination. A session extends the latter concept and encompasses bidirectional communication between two endpoints, thus capturing the entire packet exchange over time. This paper addresses session-level classification, as it provides a comprehensive understanding of network behavior, especially in identifying encrypted traffic patterns \cite{wang2017end, centime_maonan2021centime, attention_spatio_hu2023network, zou2024novel_residual}. 

Another important aspect consists in the preliminary processing of packets; this process aims to ensure that only relevant information feeds the neural network. Several approaches in the literature \cite{flowpic2_horowicz2024self, shapira2021flowpic,bu2020encrypted_parallel_packet,yao2019identification} considered both accuracy and computational costs. 

Finally, one should consider that the varying levels of complexity in the overall design problem can make certain strategies more feasible than others. The research presented in this paper analyzes the interaction between those factors from an empirical viewpoint, and provides the reader with a set of practical design guidelines.   



The paper presents a solution that relies on hardware-aware neural architecture search (HW-NAS) for network TC. The use of HW-NAS to optimize DNNs for processing raw encrypted data introduces a novel approach to cybersecurity and network traffic analysis.
HW-NAS has been successfully applied in a variety of deep learning (DL) domains \cite{1000papersNAS}\cite{chitty2023neural_ieee-access}; the application considered in this paper deals with a significant, uncovered aspect in the literature, that is, the design of optimized neural models that satisfy strict hardware constraints and yet maintain satisfactory classification performances across diverse network environments. HW-NAS can account for limitations in memory, computational power, and energy consumption, thus making real-world deployment on IoT (edge) platforms feasible.


The main contributions of this paper are: 

\begin{itemize}

\item \textbf{Hardware-aware deep networks for Traffic Classification:} To the best of the authors' knowledge, this work represents the first attempt to generate hardware-efficient deep networks that fit hard, IoT-compatible constraints. HW-NAS specifically targets session-level TC, and endows DNNs with hardware efficiency and satisfactory classification accuracy; previous approaches only focused on maximizing accuracy \cite{wang2017end,cui2019session,lu2021iclstm,only_header_cui2022only,seydali2023cbs,attention_spatio_hu2023network,zou2024novel_residual}.



\item \textbf{Header-level preprocessing and session length reduction:} This work extends existing studies by covering multiple header-level preprocessing methods, such as IP/MAC handling, UDP padding, and anonymization, alongside session-length reductions, on the optimized model. Experimental evidence demonstrates that the model achieves stable performance, maintaining high accuracy with session lengths reduced by up to 75\%, while achieving greater efficiency for resource-constrained devices through these reductions. At the same time, excessive data obfuscation or aggressive reductions in session lengths can affect accuracy, hence careful selection of configurations is required to avoid drops of up to 7\%. The paper gives practical guidelines for optimizing preprocessing pipelines and session length choices, providing a basis for future research in trading-off accuracy and resource efficiency.



\item \textbf{Efficiency benchmarks:} Established benchmarks validate the HW-NAS-driven strategy against state-of-the-art methods. The proposed model achieves substantial resource savings, with reductions of up to 224-fold in parameters, 312-fold in FLOPs, and 15-fold in maximum tensor size compared to baseline models. These reductions streamline the workflow of session-level TC by minimizing computational overhead and resource demands.





\item \textbf{Comprehensive validation across tasks:} The HW-NAS-optimized model, tailored on the ISCX VPN-nonVPN dataset \cite{iscxvpnnonvpn}, demonstrates robust performance across diverse TC tasks. It achieves 96.59\% accuracy in the main VPN-nonVPN classification task. Additionally, it delivers high accuracy in VPN differentiation (99.95\%), VPN-type classification (99.19\%), broader traffic categories (96.94\%), and session-level application identification (94.61\%), confirming its flexibility and effectiveness in real-world scenarios.


\end{itemize}






\section{Related Work}

\subsection{Traditional Approaches}

Traditional TC methods include port-based classification and DPI. These techniques have been effective in earlier network environments, but they fail when dealing with encryption and sophisticated obfuscation methods.



Port-based classification associates traffic with specific services or applications based on port numbers. %
This approach exhibits limited time complexity and proves suitable for real-time classification in older network architectures, as discussed in \cite{survey_DONG2024128444}, but it fails in modern networks due to the use of dynamic and private ports, as well as techniques such as port obfuscation and HTTP tunneling \cite{ports_dai2023glads,survey_DONG2024128444}. Other studies \cite{ports_sen2004accurate,ports_moore2005toward} showed that, in modern protocols, the default port numbers used by Peer-to-Peer (P2P) protocols only account for about 30\% of the overall traffic, thus confirming the increasing ineffectiveness of this method.



DPI inspects the payload of packets and identifies specific protocols and applications by detecting patterns or signatures within the packet contents \cite{survey_DONG2024128444}. It is effective on unencrypted traffic, but it fails when coping with encrypted communication flows because the payload is inaccessible. Furthermore, DPI is resource-intensive \cite{dpi_hongke2022dpi} and faces privacy concerns \cite{signature_dpi1_wang2020automatic}.




\subsection{Machine Learning Methods}

Conventional machine learning (ML) approaches to the classification of encrypted traffic infer patterns from statistical features of network traffic (e.g., packet size, flow duration, and inter-arrival times) without examining packet payloads \cite{survey_DONG2024128444}.


\cite{ml_skype_dong2019retracted} presented a Naïve Bayes algorithm to classify Skype traffic, and addressed the challenges in real-time processing in the presence of limited labeling. Likewise, k-NN and decision trees could apply to classify traffic based on flow characteristics\cite{iscxvpnnonvpn}, showing reasonable accuracy. In addition, \cite{ml_app_supervised_shim2017application} developed an application-level classifier by distinguishing payload size signatures, and \cite{ml_rf_supervised_zhai2018random} employed Random Forests (RF) in software-defined networking (SDN).



Addressing the issue of limited labeled data, \cite{flowpic2_horowicz2024self} converted traffic sessions into FlowPic images and applied a self‑supervised contrastive (SimCLR) framework, followed by few‑shot classification, to learn robust representations. Likewise, \cite{6476080} proposed a compound method that combined flow label propagation with compound classification to detect unknown flows and boost accuracy.


Some limitations affect traditional ML methods for TC. They rely on manual feature extraction, which is both time consuming and error prone, and even robust models such as RF require frequent retraining to maintain satisfactory performance \cite{survey_DONG2024128444}.

DL models automatically extract complex features from raw traffic data, thus bypassing the need for manual feature selection. Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) and Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) attain remarkable classification accuracy. Those models, however, tend to be computationally intensive, thus hindering their deployment in resource-constrained applications.


Several studies focused on session-level data. In \cite{wang2017end}, a 1D-CNN and 2D-CNN tested that kind of benchmark: the 1D-CNN scored an accuracy of 86.6\% and outperformed the 2D-CNN. The approach presented in \cite{wang2017malware} also used 1D-CNN for malware TC. \cite{he2020image} proposed an image-based approach that converted the initial non-zero payload sessions into gray-scale images for classification using CNNs. Although it achieved high F1 scores (97.73\% for conventional encrypted traffic and 99.55\% for VPN traffic), the coarse-grained handling of subflows disregarded relevant session information. The framework in \cite{wang2020encrypted} combined CNN and Stacked AutoEncoders (SAE) for encrypted traffic classification (ETC) by integrating trimmed raw traffic data and statistical features; the method obtained a F1-score of 98\%, at the expense of the additional time required for feature extraction. The Inception-LSTM (ICLSTM) model \cite{lu2021iclstm} converted traffic data into gray-scale images and applied Inception and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks; it attained accuracy higher than 98\%, but it exhibited some limitations in adapting to diverse real-world scenarios. The integration of LSTM, CNN, and a squeeze-and-excitation module to capture spatiotemporal features \cite{attention_spatio_hu2023network} achieved over 90\% accuracy on encrypted and unencrypted traffic relying on end-to-end representation learning. Focusing on attention mechanisms added a computational overhead and limits its scalability for resource-constrained environments.


At the packet level, \cite{lotfollahi2020deep} introduced \textit{Deep Packet}, which classified encrypted traffic using a combination of SAE and CNN. Packet-level approaches require large volumes of data, thus leading to high computational requirements and extended training times. The work in \cite{datanetwang2018} proposed \textit{DataNets}, which used MLP, SAE, and CNN models for the accurate categorization of encrypted traffic in smart home networks. Aggressive undersampling allowed to address imbalance datasets but affects the generalization performance of the model. The authors in \cite{wang2020deep_packet_malicious}  proposed a deep hierarchical neural network for packet-level malicious traffic detection; a 1D-CNN for spatial feature extraction and GRU for temporal features scored high accuracy levels across multiple intrusion detection datasets \cite{iscx2012,cicids2017}.


Several works integrate session and packet-level data.  Capsule Networks (CapsNet) \cite{cui2019session} yielded a session packet-based model for ETC. It privileged classification performance at the expense of computational overhead. Likewise, the CBS model in \cite{seydali2023cbs} combined 1D-CNN, attention-based Bi-LSTM, and SAE for ETC at both session and packet levels, by integrating spatial, temporal, and statistical features. CBS employed Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) for data augmentation and class imbalance handling, resulting in improved performance. The computational requirements limited its use in offline processing and made the method unsuitable for real-time applications. Other works \cite{yao2019identification} applied attention-based LSTM and Hierarchical Attention Networks (HAN) to classify flows as time-series data. The attention mechanism captures the most significant features, achieving 91.2\% accuracy. Similarly, \cite{shapira2021flowpic} proposed transforming packet flows into images (FlowPics) to capture size and time distributions. The approach attains high accuracy but also requires feature extraction once flows are formed—an extra step that introduces delays not encountered when working directly on raw traffic.

\subsection{Trends in Hardware-Aware NAS}
NAS methods improve model performance by tuning the network architecture on the target data. Many approaches have been proposed in the literature \cite{zoph2016neural-rl_nas,liu2018darts-gradient,evolutionary_chen2019renas,random_li2020random,knowlege-distillation_gou2021knowledge,bayesian_white2021bananas}, each improving architecture exploration in unique ways.


HW-NAS extended NAS by optimizing architectures not only for performance but also for specific hardware constraints, such as memory, latency, and computational efficiency \cite{hw-nas_survey_benmeziane2021comprehensive}. A recent work applied NAS to ETC \cite{nas_etc_malekghaini2023automl4etc}, although without strict IoT-centric constraints.
Studies such as \cite{garavagno2024affordable, sinha2024multi_hw-nas, ragusa2024combining, ragusa2024compression} illustrated how HW-NAS could produce models tailored to efficient deployment on resource-limited platforms, by matching the network design process with the requirements of the target hardware platforms.

NAS effectiveness relies on careful adaptation to the specific constraints and priorities of the target application domain. The research presented in this paper relies on HW-NAS to develop a hardware-efficient neural network for ETC. The target environment includes IoT applications, in which high accuracy and resource efficiency are of paramount importance.



\section{Preliminaries}



NAS automates neural network design by applying a search algorithm to identify optimal architectures within a predefined search space; this approach outperforms traditional hand-made models \cite{chitty2023neural_ieee-access, nas_etc_malekghaini2023automl4etc} in terms of accuracy.

Conventional NAS algorithms aim to optimize computational performance but often overlook hardware constraints; as a result, the models they produce may lack the efficiency required for their deployment in resource-limited devices. HW-NAS overcomes this limitation by directly including hardware constraints in the search process; this makes it possible to envision deployment on a variety of application devices, such as microcontroller units (MCUs), field-programmable gate arrays (FPGAs), and cloud accelerators. The extended optimization problem takes into account multiobjective constraints, including accuracy, memory usage, computational complexity (measured in FLOPs), and energy efficiency \cite{1000papersNAS}. 


The  HW-NAS optimization problem is to identify an architecture \( a \in \mathcal{A} \) that minimizes the validation loss on a given dataset \( \mathcal{D} \). Formally, this objective can be expressed as follows: 

\begin{mini}|s|
    {a \in \mathcal{A}} {\mathcal{L}_{\text{val}}(w^*(a), a)}
    {}{}
    \addConstraint{w^*(a) = \argmin_w \mathcal{L}_{\text{train}}(w, a)}
    \addConstraint{\psi(a, HW) < \text{Thr}}
    \label{eq:optnas}
\end{mini}
where \(\mathcal{A}\) denotes the search space containing all candidate architectures, \( \mathcal{L}_{\text{val}} \) represents the validation loss, and $\mathcal{L}_{\text{train}}$ is the training loss minimized to optimize the model weights \( w^*(a) \).  \( \psi(a, HW) \) is a function that measures the performance of the architecture with respect to hardware-specific metrics, and \( \text{Thr} \) is a threshold value associated with these constraints. 

Three main phases characterize NAS, namely, defining the search space, selecting the search strategy, and evaluating the performance. The search space defines the set of admissible network structures (including layer configurations and optimization hyperparameters), which collectively determine the exploration boundaries for the NAS algorithm. The search strategy then rules how the NAS algorithm explores the architecture space. 
Finally, the performance evaluation tests the candidate architecture using a task-specific merit function, which adapts to the requirements of the application.

\section{Methodology: design of efficient network for traffic classification}


The method described in this paper provides a novel design pipeline to develop lightweight, optimized DNN architecture for session-level ETC.









The workflow consists of three key stages: (1) \textbf{Data preprocessing} arranges raw traffic data to suit DL applications, preserving critical features for robust model performance; (2) in \textbf{HW-NAS execution}, the search algorithm explores the space of admissible architectures, aiming to work out an optimal-performing model that yet satisfies hardware constraints; finally, (3) the \textbf{Model selection and testing} task first picks out the best-performing architecture, based on performance and constraints, then completes a rigorous testing process involving multiple tasks and configurations. %



\subsection{Preprocessing}


The following steps implement the preprocessing phase.%

\textbf{Session extraction}: Raw traffic data is divided into sessions; each session consists of an ordered sequence of bidirectional packets exchanged between the same source and destination IP addresses, with matching port numbers and protocols. Packet data is typically stored as raw bytes. %

\textbf{Data cleaning}: This task removes data-link layer information, including MAC, and anonymizes IP addresses. This approach is frequently adopted in the literature to prevent overfitting; otherwise, some session-specific features could lead the model to memorize traffic patterns, instead of learning generalization-relevant features \cite{lotfollahi2020deep,seydali2023cbs}.

\textbf{Filtering irrelevant data}: Packets without payloads (e.g., those featuring SYN, ACK, or FIN flags) and irrelevant DNS segments are discarded. Prior studies showed that those kinds of packets did not contribute meaningfully to TC, and could actually add noise to the dataset. Filtering these packets ensures to use of only meaningful traffic data for model training \cite{seydali2023cbs}.

\textbf{Session normalization}: in compliance with a method originally proposed in \cite{wang2017end} and \cite{wang2017malware}, each session is normalized to a uniform length of 784 bytes. This approach proved effective in handling varying session sizes, while ensuring consistency for model input. Longer sessions are truncated, and shorter sessions are padded with null bytes to maintain a uniform length over all sessions.

\textbf{Data scaling}: This process prepares the data for subsequent input to the model, converting raw byte information into a standardized format suitable for neural network training. Each byte in the raw packet values forming session data is normalized into the range [0,1].




\subsection{Optimization Problem in HW-NAS} \label{optimization}


To maximize generalization performance and meet hardware constraints, the HW-NAS design strategy implies an optimization problem, which can be formalized as follows:

\begin{maxi}|s|
    {a \in \mathcal{A}} {\text{Accuracy}_{\text{val}}(w^*(a), a)}
    {}{}
    \addConstraint{w^*(a) = \argmin_w \mathcal{L}_{\text{train}}(w, a)}
    \addConstraint{|P| < F_{\text{Th}}}
    \addConstraint{|T| < R_{\text{Th}}}
    \addConstraint{\text{Flops} < \text{Flops}_{\text{Th}}}
    \label{eq:optprob}
\end{maxi}


Hardware constraints are applied using thresholds $F_{\text{Th}}$, $R_{\text{Th}}$, and $\text{Flops}_{\text{Th}}$, which manage the critical resource limitations typically characterizing IoT devices. These thresholds stem from existing works, such as \cite{ragusa2024combining}.


The parameter-count threshold, $F_{\text{Th}}$, controls the total number of model parameters, and directly affects memory usage. Reducing that quantity minimizes the model’s memory footprint, and helps fit the limited Flash memory available on edge devices. The threshold on the maximum tensor size, $R_{\text{Th}}$, manages intermediate data generated during inference, and ensures that it fits the available RAM. This is critical for session-level classification tasks, where efficient memory utilization is essential to avoid overflows during computation. %
Finally, the FLOPs threshold, $\text{Flops}_{\text{Th}}$, limits computational complexity by bounding the number of floating-point operations, which serves as a proxy for inference speed and energy consumption. This constraint is essential for maintaining real-time performance on devices that often lack dedicated GPUs.



\subsection{Search Space Design}


The HW-NAS search space in this work is a block-wise structure relying on 1D CNNs, which are highly efficient in capturing the dependencies in session-based traffic data. They extract localized structural patterns within individual packets (e.g., payload and headers), timing features (e.g., inter-packet arrival times), and sequential trends across an entire session (e.g., bidirectional flows). Recurrent neural networks (RNNs), such as LSTMs and GRUs, can model sequential dependencies in session-based traffic data. At the same time, their step-by-step processing paradigm increases computational overhead and training time, making them unsuitable for real-time applications. Likewise, Transformers capture global relationships using self-attention, but exhibit impractical memory and computational requirements for resource-constrained environments. In contrast, 1D CNNs can benefit from weight sharing and local connectivity to extract features efficiently, yielding a balance between computational cost and classification accuracy. 



Each convolutional layer in the CNN block is defined by its kernel size, number of filters, padding, and stride. These parameters allow the model to flexibly adjust its receptive field and depth so that it can capture both detailed packet-level features and overall session-level patterns.
A series of batch normalization, ReLU activation, pooling (max or average, with adjustable pooling size), and dropout (with a configurable dropout rate) follow each convolution layer. This improves feature representation while minimizing computational load. A Global Average Pooling (GAP) layer supports the final feature aggregation, which condenses spatial information into a compact representation and feeds a dense layer for the eventual classification outcome.


\subsection{Evolutionary Algorithm for NAS Search}

In the proposed HW-NAS framework, an evolutionary algorithm supports the exploration of architecture space.  Evolutionary algorithms proved to be highly effective toward that purpose \cite{1000papersNAS}. This approach is suitable to ensure that target architectures meet predefined hardware requirements \cite{ragusa2024combining}.


The search strategy starts from an initial (parent) architecture, $a_0$. A mutation function $R_m(a_p)$ then spawns candidate architectures (children). Mutations involve random modifications to the parent, such as adding new blocks with randomly generated hyperparameters, removing blocks to reduce complexity, or adjusting parameters within existing blocks (e.g., number of filters, kernel size). Each child is then evaluated against hardware constraints, and only those architectures that satisfy the HW criteria are allowed to go to training.

This spawning/selection process iterates over a predefined number of generations $N_g$; each generation yields $N_c$ child architectures. If a child fails the hardware constraint check, further mutations apply until the required number of admissible children is achieved. A maximum depth constraint limits the number of blocks within each architecture, ensuring that each child remains within a preset complexity level. 

After passing the hardware check, each child undergoes training on the training dataset $\mathcal{X_T}$, and is subsequently evaluated for validation accuracy on the validation set $\mathcal{X_V}$. The architecture with the highest validation accuracy becomes the new parent, $a_p$, for the next generation. 











