The VPN-NonVPN experiment is chosen as the baseline for HW-NAS optimization due to its comprehensive nature and frequent use in previous research, which provides a solid foundation for comparisons with state-of-the-art models.
This task encompasses both VPN and Non-VPN traffic, capturing shared features relevant to other experiments. Running HW-NAS on this task generates an architecture that generalizes effectively across various tasks, ensuring resource-efficient optimization without the need for task-specific designs.

The HW-NAS, executed on a workstation with a Nvidia 2080 Ti GPU, receives preprocessed session data as input, which are extracted and prepared using Scapy, a Python library for packet manipulation. The HW-NAS codebase was built with Keras and TensorFlow.


The thresholds for memory usage, FLOPs, and RAM are defined based on the minimum values observed among session-level TC models in prior works. These metrics are calculated following details provided by each study and implemented in Keras to ensure consistency and accuracy.


A validation set consisting of 20\% of the training data is created using a standard holdout method. Architectures are trained for up to 100 epochs, with an initial learning rate of $10^{-3}$, a batch size of 128, and a learning rate reduction triggered by a plateau in validation loss. Early stopping is applied using the validation loss as the stopping criterion. Networks are trained 3 times using a multi-start approach. 


The HW-NAS process spans 100 generations, with each generation containing a population of 10 candidate architectures (children). The search space includes filters ranging from 16 to 140, kernel sizes from 3 to 7, strides from 1 to 6, and dropout rates from 0.1 to 0.5. Pooling, if enabled, can be either max or average, with a pool size between 2 and 3. Padding is set as either “same” or “valid.” The best-performing model is selected based on validation accuracy.


The model generated by the NAS is then retrained across all experiments in Table \ref{tab:experiment_descriptions} to assess its generalization performance. For these evaluations, the model is trained for up to 200 epochs with early stopping.

