\section{Conclusion and Future Work}

This paper introduces DNN optimized through HW-NAS for efficient TC in resource-constrained environments. The proposed model achieves up to 96.59\% accuracy in session-level classification while requiring up to 444 times fewer parameters and 312 times fewer FLOPs than state-of-the-art methods. These results highlight its suitability for IoT and edge platforms, where efficiency is critical.

The proposed model delivers robust performance across a variety of tasks and configurations, showcasing its flexibility and adaptability to different TC challenges. The study shows that reducing input size enhances compute efficiency, enabling deployment in environments with stricter hardware constraints without sacrificing performance. Additionally, techniques like UDP padding mitigate accuracy loss in restrictive preprocessing scenarios, offering practical solutions.

Future work will focus on deploying the proposed model in real-world, latency-sensitive environments, including low-power IoT devices and edge-based network management systems. Moreover, federated learning paradigms will be explored to enable decentralized training while preserving privacy and improving scalability. We also plan to extend HW-NAS applications to other traffic analysis tasks, such as intrusion detection and anomaly detection to assess its adaptability across diverse domains. These directions aim to advance hardware-efficient ML for next-generation network systems.





