\subsection{Burrows-Wheeler Transform}\label{sec:bwt}

The \emph{Burrows-Wheeler Transform (\BWT)}~\cite{bwt94} is a reversible text transformation that permutes the characters of a given input string. Given a string $s$, \BWT produces the pair ($\bwt(s)$, $I$), where $\bwt(s)$ is obtained by concatenating the last symbols of each element of the list of all lexicographically sorted cyclic rotations of $s$, and $I$ is the position of $s$ in this list.  The permutation $\bwt(s)$ can be computed from the conjugate array $\CA$, since for all $i=1,\ldots, n$, 
$\bwt(s)[i]=s[\CA[i] - 1]$, assuming that $s[0]=s[n]$. It is easy to verify that if a string $u$ is a conjugate of $s$, then $\bwt(u)=\bwt(s)$. The index $I$ is fundamental for recovering $s$ from $\bwt(s)$, otherwise the original string can be recovered up to conjugates.


In many applications, an end-of-string character (typically represented as $\$$) is appended to the input string. This character does not belong to $\Sigma$ and is assumed to be lexicographically smaller than all characters in $\Sigma$. Since $s\$$ contains exactly one occurrence of $\$$, the transformation becomes reversible without the need for the additional index $I$. Furthermore, appending $\$$ to the string $s$ makes computing the conjugate array $\CA$ of $s\$$ equivalent to computing its suffix array $\SA$. This strategy has traditionally been used to achieve a linear-time computation of \BWT by using a linear-time suffix array construction algorithm \cite{}.  However, it is possible to compute 
$\bwt(s)$ without appending any end-of-string symbol $\$$ to $s$. In fact, this can be performed either by computing the suffix array of the Lyndon rotation of $s$ \cite{GRS07} or by using the \cais\ algorithm, which is based on induced sorting \cite{BCLRS_SPIRE2021_ebwt}.


The \emph{LF-mapping} is a map from symbols occurring in the transformed string $L$ to the string $F$ of lexicographically sorted symbols of $L$.\\
