The first example shown in Table \ref{tab:unreliable_example} demonstrates a case of low reliability in LLM annotation (Inaccurate and Unreliable). The LLaMa-3.1 8B model assigns nearly identical probabilities to all three categories (approximately 1/3 each), indicating it cannot meaningfully distinguish between them for this paper. The resulting Reliability Score of 0 signifies that the probability distribution is effectively uniform, equivalent to random guessing. Despite this uncertainty, if forced to choose, the model would incorrectly label this as either "Technical Advance" or "New Finding" rather than the expert-assigned "Interesting Hypothesis" category. This case illustrates how accuracy metrics alone would flag this as an incorrect annotation, but the proposed reliability score additionally reveals that the model has no meaningful confidence in its answer.

