    We propose label delay training, a novel approach to reduce errors in standalone speech endpointers without additional features or pause labels. Recent multi-stream speech language models have used delayed tokens \cite{kharitonov-etal-2022-text,copet2024simple,moshi} to reduce dependencies between parallel output streams. In contrast, we apply label delay to a single output stream, specifically to optimize the latency-accuracy trade-off and enhance endpointer performance.
    By shifting target labels, we encourage delayed, high-confidence predictions, which leads to inherently fewer errors. 

    % We propose an alternative approach to reduce errors while training a standalone speech endpointer without additional features or pause labels. We leverage the latency-accuracy trade-off by encouraging delayed high-confidence predictions, which allows operation at a higher decision threshold, inherently reducing potential errors. This slight latency increase is achieved through a modified training objective using label delay – shifting target labels by a few frames. Inspired by recent work in multi-stream spoken language modeling using delayed tokens \cite{kharitonov-etal-2022-text,copet2024simple,moshi}, where delay manages parallel stream dependencies, our work explores a different application. We use label delay on a single output stream to manage the latency-accuracy trade-off and enhance endpointer performance.

