\subsection{Transfer Function of Multi-band RAREs}
\subsubsection{Transfer function at the IF band}
Building on the derived quantum state solution, we can now derive the transfer function of multi-band RAREs by following the mapping flow outlined in \eqref{eq:mapping}. To elaborate, substituting \eqref{eq:state} into \eqref{eq:laserpower} yields the following explicit expression for the measured probe-laser power
\begin{align}\label{eq:y1}
    y(t) &= \mathcal{P}(\boldsymbol{\Omega}) = P_{\rm in}\exp\left(-\frac{ \chi_0 \sum_{n=1}^N\Omega_n^2}{\sum_{n=1}^N\Omega_n^2 + \Gamma^2}\right),\\&= P_{\rm in}\exp\left(-\frac{  \chi_0\sum_{n=1}^N|\Omega_{r,n} + \Omega_{s,n}e^{j(\delta_nt + \phi_n)}|^2}{\sum_{n=1}^N|\Omega_{r,n} + \Omega_{s,n}e^{j(\delta_nt + \phi_n)}|^2 + \Gamma^2}\right),\notag
\end{align}
where $\chi_0 \overset{\Delta}{=}\rho_0 C_0$. This transfer function explicitly unveils the nonlinear relationship between the laser power and the sum-square of the multi-band signals.  Such nonlinearity makes it challenging to decipher the data signals $E_{s,n},\forall n$, especially their phase information. To overcome this challenge, the reference sources are typically placed in close proximity to the RARE, ensuring that the reference signal is orders of magnitude stronger than the data signal, e.g., $|E_{r,n}| \gg |E_{s,n}|$ and $\Omega_{r,n}\gg \Omega_{s,n}$~\cite{Rydphase_Holloway2019}. 
{Under this strong-reference regime, the nonlinear transfer function in \eqref{eq:y1} can be accurately linearized via first-order Taylor expansion, greatly simplifying signal detection. Specifically, the derivative of $\mathcal{P}(\boldsymbol{\Omega})$ with {respect} to (w.r.t.) $\Omega_{n},\forall n$ is expressed as: 
\begin{align}\label{eq:gradient}
    \frac{\partial \mathcal{P}(\boldsymbol{\Omega})}{\partial \Omega_n} = \mathcal{P}(\boldsymbol{\Omega})\frac{2\chi_0\Gamma^2\Omega_n}{(\sum_{m=1}^N\Omega_m^2 + \Gamma^2)^2}. 
\end{align}
Given \eqref{eq:gradient}, the linearized laser-power expression is presented in \eqref{eq:y2}.}
