\subsubsection{Transfer function at the digital baseband}
After obtaining the probe-laser power from the PD, digital baseband processing is conducted to convert the IF signal components into the digital baseband. 
To prevent inter-band interference, it is essential to make the IF components, $\Re{E_{s,n}e^{j(\delta_n t + \phi_n)}}$, mutually orthogonal. To this end, the 
% frequency gap
{separation}
between adjacent IFs, $\frac{\delta_{n + 1} - \delta_n}{2\pi}$, should exceed the average occupied bandwidth, 
$\frac{B_n + B_{n + 1}}{2}$, by an adequate safe margin. For instance, we can set $N = 3$, $\delta_1 = 2\pi\times100\:{\rm kHz}$, $\delta_2 = 2\pi\times200\:{\rm kHz}$, $\delta_3 = 2\pi\times300\:{\rm kHz}$, and $B_1 = B_2 = B_3 = 80\:{\rm kHz}$. Under this orthogonal condition, the data signal at each frequency band can be independently captured using band-pass filters and I/Q demodulators, as shown in the right part of Fig.~\ref{img:RARE}. By further considering the system noise, the baseband signal originating from the $n$-th frequency band is thus obtained as 
\begin{align}
    y_n = \kappa_n E_{s,n} + z_n,\:\forall n\in\{1,2,\cdots, N\}.
\end{align}
% where $z_n$ denotes the AWGN at band $n$. 
% Based on $y_n$, we can conduct diverse multi-band CommunSense tasks, which will be discussed in detail in Section IV.
{\color{black} Here, $z_n$ denotes the noise, whose distribution will be discussed in next sub-section.}
{The outputs $\{y_n\}$ enable multi-band CommunSense applications, as detailed in Section~\ref{sec:4}.}

\subsection{Multi-Band Noise Characterization}
In the final part of this section, we characterize the noise affecting the detected signal $y_n$ by modifying the single-band model \cite{RydbergNoise_2024}. Following \cite{RydbergNoise_2024}, the noise, $z_n$, comprises two key components: \emph{intrinsic noise} and \emph{extrinsic noise}.

The intrinsic noise is largely attributed to the photon shot noise (PSN) of the PD~\cite{RydbergNoise_2024}. {\color{black} The equivalent noise power across bandwidth $B_n$ for band $n$ is proportional to the DC bias of probe laser, $\mathcal{P}_r$, resulting from the multi-band reference signals as we derived in \eqref{eq:y2}.} Accordingly, it is derived as 
\begin{align}
    \sigma^2_{{\rm I}, n} = \mathcal{P}_r B_n {\hbar\omega_p}\overset{\Delta}{=}\mathcal{P}_rB_nC_1,
\end{align}

The extrinsic noise primarily arises from the black-body radiation at an ambient temperature $T_{\rm a}$~\cite{RydbergNoise_2024}. It contaminates the wireless data in the free space, and is thereby amplified by the gain of RARE. {\color{black} In the considered multi-band RARE system, the extrinsic noise model is based on modifying the amplified single-band gain factor adopted in \cite{RydbergNoise_2024} by the derived band-specific gain factor, $\kappa_n$.} Then, the power of extrinsic noise at band $n$ is derived as
\begin{align}
    \sigma^2_{{\rm E},n} = \kappa_n^2B_n\frac{4\hbar f_n^3\left(e^{\hbar\omega_n/k_BT_a} + 1\right)}{\epsilon_0c^3 \left(e^{\hbar\omega_n/k_BT_a} - 1\right)}\overset{\Delta}{=}\kappa_n^2B_nC_2,
\end{align}
where $c$ the speed of light, $\epsilon_0$ the dielectric constant, and $k_B$ the Boltzmann constant.




% where $T_e$ the environment temperature.



Based on the preceding analysis, the noise, $z_n$, can be modeled as an additive white
Gaussian noise obeying $z_n \sim \mathcal{CN}(0,\sigma_n^2)$, where
\begin{align}
    \sigma_n^2 = \sigma^2_{{\rm I}, n} + \sigma^2_{{\rm E},n} = \mathcal{P}_rB_nC_1 + \kappa_n^2B_nC_2.  
\end{align}

\section{Sensitivity Analysis and Maximization for Multi-Band RAREs}\label{sec:3}
In this section, we maximize the sensitivity of a multi-band RARE by optimizing the multi-band atomic amplifier through the manipulation of 
the Rabi frequencies of reference signals. To this end, the explicit expression of the sensitivity is derived, analyzed, and then used to obtain the optimal Rabi frequencies.
\subsection{Sensitivity Analysis}
