\subsection{Sensitivity Maximization} 
The optimal Rabi sum-square {$\mathcal{A}=\sum_{n=1}^N\Omega_{r,n}^2$} is derived in the sequel {to maximize the normalized global gain}  $\frac{\varrho_0^2}{\mathcal{P}_r}$, {thereby achieving} the maximal {sensitivity across} all frequency bands. 
% It is important to note that 
{Notably,} the optimization of the Rabi attentions {$\{\alpha_n\}$} is 
% significantly influenced by the performance metrics employed in downstream communication and sensing tasks, which will be addressed in the next section.
{contingent on application-specific performance metrics for CommunSense, which are detailed in Section~\ref{sec:4}.}
To be specific, the sensitivity maximization problem is formulated as 
\begin{align}\label{eq:P0}
    \max_{\mathcal{A} > 0}\:\frac{\varrho_0^2}{\mathcal{P}_r},
\end{align}
where 
\begin{align}\label{eq:objective}
    \frac{\varrho_0^2}{\mathcal{P}_r} = \frac{4\chi_0^2\Gamma^4 P_{\rm in}}{\hbar^2}\exp\left(-\frac{\chi_0\mathcal{A}}{\mathcal{A} + \Gamma^2}\right)\frac{\mathcal{A}}{(\mathcal{A} + \Gamma^2)^4}.
\end{align}
% The global optimal solution to \eqref{eq:P0} is provided in Theorem 2.
{The objective function in \eqref{eq:objective} comprises a constant, an exponential decay term, and a rational function of $\mathcal{A}$. Theorem~2 provides the closed-form global optimum for \eqref{eq:P0}, which governs the fundamental sensitivity limits of the multi-band RARE.}
\begin{theorem}
    The optimal $\mathcal{A}$ that solves the problem in \eqref{eq:P0} is given as 
    \begin{align}\label{eq:A}
        \mathcal{A}^\star =  \frac{\chi_0+4 - \sqrt{\chi_0^2 + 4\chi_0 + 16}}{\chi_0 -4 + \sqrt{\chi_0^2 + 4\chi_0 + 16}}\Gamma^2. 
    \end{align}
    % When $\mathcal{A} < \mathcal{A}^\star$, the function $\frac{\varrho_0^2} {\mathcal{P}_r}$ is monotonically increasing, while when $\mathcal{A} > \mathcal{A}^\star$, $\frac{\varrho_0^2}{\mathcal{P}_r}$ is monotonically decreasing.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
    (See Appendix~\ref{app:proof_sumsquare}). 
\end{proof}
{The function $\frac{\varrho_0^2} {\mathcal{P}_r}$ is monotonically increasing for $\mathcal{A} < \mathcal{A}^\star$ and monotonically decreasing for $\mathcal{A} > \mathcal{A}^\star$.}
Utilizing Theorem 2, the normalized global gain $(\frac{\varrho_0}{\mathcal{P}_r})^{\star}$  that maximizes sensitivity is acquired. By substituting $\mathcal{A}^\star$ into \eqref{eq:laserpower2} and \eqref{eq:Global_gain}, we can also obtain the corresponding DC bias and global gain, denoted as $\mathcal{P}_r^{\star}$ and $\varrho_0^{\star}$ respectively. 
Furthermore, Theorem 2 reveals that for a multi-band RARE, regardless the number of frequency bands considered, the sum square of the multi-band Rabi frequencies of the reference signals, $\mathcal{A} = \sum_{n = 1}^N\Omega_{r,n}^2$, must be $\mathcal{A}^{\star}$. 
{Since $\Omega_{r,n} = \frac{\mu_n}{\hbar}|E_{r,n}|$ is proportional to the incident electric field, this optimal condition implies a fixed weighted sum of received reference-signal powers: \begin{align}
    \sum_{n = 1}^N\frac{\mu_n^2}{\hbar^2}|E_{r,n}|^2=\text{constant.}
\end{align}}

Nevertheless, while the Rabi sum-square is fixed, we can still control the distribution of Rabi frequencies $\left\{\frac{\Omega_{r,n}^2}{\sum_{m=1}^N\Omega_{r,m}^2}\right\}$ to flexibly manipulate the Rabi attentions $\{\alpha_n\}$, thereby yielding degrees-of-freedom for further improving the CommunSense performance.

