well with the exact solutions for the cases $N = 1,2,4,8$, validating the efficacy of our signal model. However, when $N = 16$ or $N = 32$, the theoretical waveforms slightly deviate from the exact solutions. This derivation stems from the narrow instantaneous bandwidth of a RARE~\cite{jia_properties_2024}. Specifically, when $N$ is small, the maximum IF (e.g., $\delta_8/2\pi = 800\:{\rm kHz}$ for $N = 8$) is within the maximum instantaneous bandwidth of a RARE (typically $1\sim10\:{\rm MHz}$~\cite{jia_properties_2024}). In this context, the quantum state 
$\rho_{12}$ will have adequate time to converge to the steady-state solution 
derived in \eqref{eq:state}. On the contrary, as $N$ increases, the maximum IF (e.g., $\delta_{32}/2\pi = 3.2\:{\rm MHz}$) gradually approaches the maximum instantaneous bandwidth of a RARE, resulting in insufficient time for the electron transition to reach a steady state. This discrepancy causes the exact solution to deviate from the theoretical model. Overall, the derived signal transmission model precisely 
captures the dynamics of multi-level electron transitions when the signal bandwidth falls within the detectable instantaneous bandwidth of a RARE.  

