\subsection{Simulation on the RTL Nodes}\label{netlist}
% 我们扩展了Iverilog的concurrent fault simulation能力。由于Iverilog本身的事件调度策略，即xxxx，使得在扩展时会出现一些问题，如fake event的产生，这会影响故障仿真结果的正确性。在我们的框架中，我们通过调整RTL nodes的evaluate和schedule关系来修复该问题。
We enhance the concurrent fault simulation capabilities for all RTL nodes (logic nodes, arithmetic nodes, and others) in Iverilog. Due to Iverilog's inherent event scheduling strategy, where certain nodes are evaluated immediately upon receiving new values while others are deferred\cite{iverilog}, issues, such as fake events, arise during the extension. Those issues may undermine the accuracy of fault simulation results. In our framework, we resolve this problem by adjusting the evaluation and scheduling relationships of the RTL nodes.

% Compared to the gate-level netlist, the RTL graph contains a wider variety of node types beyond just logic operations, such as arithmetic operations, shift operations, and other. We have extended the concurrent fault simulation capabilities to cover all types of nodes in Iverilog.
% and implemented approximately 5000 lines of c++ code. 
% However, during this extension, issues such as fake events arise due to the inherent event scheduling characteristics of Iverilog. Specifically, certain nodes are evaluated immediately upon receiving new values, while other nodes that should be evaluated promptly are instead deferred. In our framework, we refine the evaluating and scheduling scheme for all nodes.
% according to the principle of \textit{"Evaluate immediately when possible, defer evaluation otherwise"}.

 % 是否需要精简一些？？？？？？？
% \textbf{Fake Events Removal.} 
% When extending the capability of fault simulation in Iverilog, the presence of event nodes may cause fake events during concurrent fault simulation. 
Fake events refer to the premature evaluation of bad gates on event nodes in Iverilog, due to the impact of good events, resulting in erroneous activation of waiting behavioral codes. 
 Because of good events are sent to their successors before bad events, which causes the bad gate of event nodes to mistakenly detect an event when receiving good values, resulting in incorrect activation of waiting behavioral codes. 
 Specifically, because good events are sent to successors before bad events, the bad gate of event nodes mistakenly detects an event when receiving good values, while the actual arrival of bad events may generate different events, resulting in incorrect activation of waiting behavioral codes.
 To avoid abnormal activation caused by fake events, we postpone the evaluation of all event nodes until all blocking events\cite{ieee_verilog} have been processed. 
 % Once all blocking events have been evaluated, we then check the event nodes to determine which behavioral code can be activated.   




% \textbf{Redundant Events or Evaluation Reduce.} 
% The scheduling of events and evaluation of nodes are critical factors that can degrade performance of RTL netlist simulation\cite{mozart}. 
% Some nodes, such as arithmetic nodes, are evaluated immediately when they receive new input values. However, this may result in redundant executions if other inputs to these nodes are still unstable. Conversely, some logic nodes, like BUF, which should ideally be evaluated immediately, are instead deferred for later evaluation, which introduces scheduling overhead. The principle of \textit{"Evaluate immediately when possible, defer evaluation otherwise"} is used to refine those.

% In Iverilog, certain nodes, such as arithmetic nodes, perform an immediate evaluation as soon as they receive new values. However, this may be redundant due to the potential instability in other inputs to these nodes. Conversely, certain logic nodes, like BUF, which should be evaluated immediately, are instead queued for later evaluation. This introduces extra scheduling overhead to some extent. In our framework, we refine the evaluating and scheduling scheme for all nodes according to the principle of \textit{"Evaluate immediately when possible, defer evaluation otherwise"}.

