\subsubsection{Algebraic Degree}
Cryptographic functions must have high algebraic degrees. Indeed, all cryptosystems using Boolean functions for confusion (combining or filtering functions in stream ciphers, functions involved in the S-boxes of block ciphers) can be attacked if the functions have low algebraic degree.
%\subsubsection{Boolean function}
A function with a high algebraic degree is more complex and generally more secure against algebraic attacks. It indicates the function's resistance to being approximated by lower-degree polynomials \cite{carlet2010boolean}. 
\begin{definition}\label{bf:AD}%(Algebraic degree) 
	Algebraic degree of a Boolean function $f$, is showed by $deg_{f}$ and is defined as the maximum degree of the polynomial in the ANF representation of $f$. 
	%Equivalently, it is defined as the maximum cardinality of $I$ with $a_{I} \neq0$ in Definition \ref{bf:ANF} \cite{picek2015applications}. The algebraic degree is affine invariant.
	%For a non constant Boolean function $f \in \mathcal{B}_{2}^{n}$, lower and upper bounds of algebraic degree are as $1 \leq deg_{_{f}} \leq n$. Also $1 \leq deg_{_{f}} < n$ if and only if $2 | Hw(f) $ \cite{burnett2005heuristic}. The maximum algebraic degree of any affine Boolean functions is equal to one.
\end{definition}
%Algebraic degree of an S-box $S$ is the maximum degree of its' component functions. Figure \ref{AD-security} shows that S-boxes with higher algebraic degrees generally providing better security \cite{claude_carlet_2020} since a higher algebraic degree indicates greater complexity and resistance to algebraic attacks, where attackers try to exploit the algebraic structure of the S-box.

%The algebraic degree $deg_{_{f}}$  imposes an upper bound on the maximum magnitude of the Walsh coefficients. Specifically if $d=deg_{_{f}}$
%$\max_{\textbf{u} \in \mathbb{F}_{2}^{n}}  | W_{f}(\textbf{u}) | \leq 2^{n-d}$.
