\subsubsection{Cycle Structure, Fixed Points and Opposite Fixed Points}
In the context of an S-Box, a fixed point is an input value that, when passed through the S-Box, produces the same output value.
% In simpler terms, if you take a number (or bit pattern), substitute it using the S-Box, and get the same number back, that number is a fixed point. This can be thought of as a point of stability in the transformation; the input and output are unchanged.
Opposite (also called inverse or negated) fixed points refer to pairs of input values that, when substituted through the S-Box, yield outputs that are complementary or “opposite” to each other in some sense. 
%The analysis of fixed points and cycle structures in S-boxes is important for preventing certain types of attacks and ensuring good statistical properties (Figure \ref{Fp-security}).
An S-box should have very few, if any fixed or opposite fixed points \cite{mishra2023searching}.

\begin{definition}\label{vbf:FP}
	The cycle structure of an invertible vector Boolean function (permutation) describes the number of cycles and their length. A cycle of $k$ numbers is referred to as a $k$-cycle or a cycle of length $k$ \cite{cubero2015vector}. 	

	For an S-box $S \in \mathcal{B}_{n}^{m}$, an input $\textbf{x}$ is a fixed point of the $S$ if $S(\textbf{x})=\textbf{x}$, and an input $\overline{\textbf{x}}$ is an opposite fixed point of the $S$ if $S(\textbf{x}) = \overline{\textbf{x}}$
\end{definition}
%A cycle structure with a low number of cycles of high length is considered well suited to be used in cipher design. This fact means that many transpositions are present \cite{cubero2015vector}.
%Also minimizing the number of fixed points and opposite fixed points ensuring a desirable cycle structure can enhance the S-box's resistance to slide attacks and certain statistical analyses \cite{biryukov2003toolbox}. While the absence of fixed points or opposite fixed points was traditionally considered desirable, modern research has shown that a small number of fixed points does not necessarily weaken the cipher. 
The number of fixed points should be minimized to enhance security. An S-box $S \in \mathcal{B}_{n}^{m}$, with no fixed points (i.e.,$FP(S)=0$ ) is ideal, as it prevents trivial attacks. In practice, S-boxes should aim for a low number of fixed points, with a common target being fewer than $2^{n-1}$ fixed points for an S-box $S$.
