\subsubsection{Nonlinearity} 
%One of the most important cryptographic properties of a boolean function is its nonlinearity. This notion has been introduced by rothaus \cite{rothaus1976bent}, before it was related to known (or publicly known) attacks on the cryptosystems.
%\subsubsection{Boolean function}
It is a measure of how far a Boolean function $f \in \mathcal{B}_{n}$ is from being an affine Boolean function and defined as the minimum Hamming distance between $f$ and all affine functions \cite{claude_carlet_2020}.  

\begin{definition}\label{bf:NL} %(Nonlinearity degree) 
	The nonlinearity of a Boolean function $f \in \mathcal{B}_{n}$ can be expressed in terms of the WHT coefficients as \cite{claude_carlet_2020}:
	\begin{equation}
	Nl_{f}=2^{n-1}-\dfrac{1}{2}\max_{\textbf{u} \in \mathbb{F}_{2}^{n}} \left| W_{f}(\textbf{u}) \right|
	\end{equation}
	%	Several bounds on nonlinearity of Boolean function $f \in \mathcal{B}_{2}^{n}$ have been established, including:
	%	\begin{itemize}
	%		\item  Sidelnikov-Chabaud-Vaudenay bound. This bound is known as the covering radius bound and is valid for every Boolean function. It is tight for every even $n$ \cite{claude_carlet_2020}. \\
	%		$Nl_{_{f}} \leq 2^{n}- 2^{\frac{n-1}{2}}$  
	%		\item  Sarkar-Maitra bound \cite{claude_carlet_2020}.\\
	%		if $AI_{_{f}} \leq n-2$ then $Nl_{_{f}} \leq 2^{n-1}- 2^{t}$
	%	\end{itemize}	
\end{definition}
%The naive method for computing nonlinearity degree of an $n$-variable Boolean function has complexity $\mathcal{O}(n.2^{n})$.	

A generalization to VBF of the notion of nonlinearity of Boolean functions has been introduced and studied by Nyberg \cite{nyberg1992construction} and further studied by Chabaud and Vaudenay \cite{chabaud1994links}.
Nonlinearity quantifies how far the S-box deviates from being an affine VBF.
It measures the distance of the S-box to the dependent functions, with higher values indicating better resistance to cryptographic linear analysis \cite{carlet2010boolean,canteaut2016lecture}. 

%Nonlinearity is a crucial property that measures the resistance of an S-box against linear cryptanalysis.
%Figure \ref{Nl-security} shows that an S-box with high nonlinearity ensures that the relationship between input and output bits is complex and difficult to approximate with linear functions.
%Recent research has explored new constructions that approach optimal nonlinearity while maintaining other critical properties \cite{carlet2020intrinsic}.

The nonlinearity of an S-box can be computed using the Walsh-Hadamard Transform (WHT). 
by definition \ref{vbf:WHT}, the minimum Hamming distance of all functions $<\textbf{v} \ldotp S(\textbf{x})>$ with affine functions $<\textbf{u} \ldotp \textbf{x}>$ is called the nonlinear degree of the S-box and is defined as follows, 
\begin{definition}\label{vbf:NL}
	The nonlinearity of $S \in \mathcal{B}_{n}^{m}$ is given by \cite{crama2010boolean},
	\begin{equation}
	NL_{S} = 2^{n-1} - \frac{1}{2} \max_{\textbf{u} \in \mathbb{F}_{2}^{n}} \left| \sum_{\textbf{x} \in \mathbb{F}_{2}^{n}} (-1)^{S(\textbf{x}) \oplus <\textbf{u} , \textbf{x}>}\right| \ , \ (\textbf{u}\neq \textbf{0})
	\end{equation}
	%	Equivalently, the nonlinearity of $S$, is defined as the minimum nonlinearity of all its component output boolean functions \cite{crama2010boolean} and their linear combinations \cite{van2014encyclopedia,picek2016evolutionary,claude_carlet_2020}.
	For an S-box $S \in \mathcal{B}_{n}^{n}$, the theoretical lower bound is $0$ and the upper bound for nonlinearity is $2^{n-1}- 2^{\frac{n}{2}}+2$ for $n$ even and $2^{n-1}- 2^{\frac{n-1}{2}}$ for $n$ odd. 
	%However, achieving this bound becomes increasingly difficult as n grows \cite{budaghyan2009constructing}. For more information about lower or upper bound of nonlinearity of different types of Boolean functions such as resilient function, Carlet-feng function, MAI function, bent function, Tu-deng function etc. see \cite{bharti2016survey}. 
\end{definition}
