Next, we implement the \emph{feasible PIR coding framework} based on Lagrange codes,  utilizing the group of elements $\{\beta_{i,k},\alpha_n:i\in[0:\lambda),k\in[0:K+X),n\in[0:N)\}$ that satisfies the constraints P0--P3 as encoding parameters. 
It is sufficient to provide the construction of the encoding functions $\{b_{i,0}(x),\ldots,b_{i,K+X-1}(x)\}_{i\in[0:\lambda)}$ and $\{v_{i,k,0}^{(\mathcal{R})}(x),\ldots,v_{i,k,T}^{(\mathcal{R})}(x)\}_{i\in\mathcal{R},k\in[0:K)}$ for any nonempty subset $\mathcal{R}\subseteq[0:\lambda)$.
The key idea behind designing these encoding functions is to construct the secure storage functions and private query functions using Lagrange interpolating polynomials.
%The key idea %behind designing PIR schemes based on Lagrange codes  
%is to construct the secure storage system and private queries using Lagrange interpolation polynomials, such that the server responses can be interpreted as evaluations of the product of the query and storage polynomials and the desired partial file can be recovered through interpolation of the resulting product polynomial.


More formally, for any $m\in[0:M)$ and $i\in[0:\lambda)$, the storage function $\tilde{f}_i^{(m)}(x)$ is constructed as a Lagrange interpolating polynomial of degree $K+X-1$, given by
\begin{IEEEeqnarray}{c}
