&&+\sum\limits_{i\in\mathcal{R}}\bigg(\sum\limits_{j\in[0:K)\backslash\{k\}}\widetilde{w}_{i,j}^{(\theta)}\cdot\frac{1}{x-\beta_{i,j}}\bigg)\bigg(\prod\limits_{j\in[0:K)\backslash\{k\}}\frac{x-\beta_{i,j}}{\beta_{i,k}-\beta_{i,j}}\bigg) \notag \\
&&+\sum\limits_{i\in\mathcal{R}}
\bigg(\prod\limits_{j\in[0:K)\backslash\{k\}}\frac{x-\beta_{i,j}}{\beta_{i,k}-\beta_{i,j}}\bigg)
\bigg(\sum\limits_{k=K}^{K+X-1}\widetilde{z}_{i,k}^{(\theta)}\cdot x^{k-K}\bigg) \notag\\
&&+\bigg(\sum\limits_{t=0}^{T-1}\widetilde{z}_{i,k,t}^{(\theta,\mathcal{R})}\cdot x^t\cdot\prod\limits_{k\in[0:K)}\left(x-\beta_{i,k}\right)\bigg)\cdot\tilde{f}_{i}^{(\theta)}(x)+\sum\limits_{m\in[0:M)\backslash\{\theta\}}\sum\limits_{i\in\mathcal{R}}\widetilde{q}_{i,k}^{(m,\mathcal{R})}(x)\cdot \tilde{f}_{i}^{(m)}(x) \IEEEeqnarraynumspace
\label{framework:answer:2}\\
&=&\sum\limits_{i\in\mathcal{R}} \widetilde{w}_{i,k}^{(\theta)}\cdot\frac{1}{x-\beta_{i,k}}+\sum\limits_{j=0}^{K+X+T-2}I_j\cdot x^j, \label{framework:answer:234}
\end{IEEEeqnarray}
where $I_j$ represents various combinations of interferences, which can be precisely determined  by expanding the equation in \eqref{framework:answer:2}, though its exact form is not crucial.
From the response function $\widetilde{A}_{k}^{(\theta,\mathcal{R})}(x)$ in \eqref{framework:answer:234} for any $k\in[0:K)$, we can find that the desired symbol $\widetilde{w}^{(\theta)}_{i,k}$ is aligned along the dimension corresponding to the Cauchy term $\frac{1}{x-\beta_{i,k}}$ for any $i\in\mathcal{R}$, and the interference $I_j$ is aligned along the dimension corresponding to the Vandermonde term $x^j$ for any $j\in[0:K+X+T-2]$.

Since $\widetilde{A}_{n,k}^{(\theta,\mathcal{R})}$ is an evaluation of $\widetilde{A}_{k}^{(\theta,\mathcal{R})}(x)$ at $x=\alpha_n$, the desired symbols $\{\widetilde{w}^{(\theta)}_{i,k}\}_{i\in\mathcal{R}}$ can be decoded from the values $\{ \widetilde{A}_{n,k}^{(\theta,\mathcal{R})},\widetilde{w}^{(\theta)}_{i,k}:n\in\Delta,i\in\mathcal{D} \}$ in \eqref{Frame:answers:lagrange} by Lemma \ref{C-V matrix pole} and P0--P3. After decoding the symbols $\{\widetilde{w}^{(\theta)}_{i,k}\}_{i\in\mathcal{R}}$ for all $k\in[0:K)$, the user will recover the partial file $\widetilde{\mathbf{W}}^{(\theta)}(\mathcal{R},:)$ by \eqref{Franmework:file:symbols}. 
Therefore, the PIR coding framework implemented using cross subspace alignment codes is feasible.

%Therefore, by \eqref{framework:answer:234} and Lemma \ref{C-V matrix pole}, the desired symbols $\{\widetilde{w}^{(\theta)}_{i,k}\}_{i\in\mathcal{R}}$ along the dimensions corresponding to the Cauchy  terms $\{\frac{1}{x-\beta_{i,k}}\}_{i\in\mathcal{R}}$ can be decoded from the values $\{ \widetilde{A}_{n,k}^{(\theta,\mathcal{R})},\widetilde{w}^{(\theta)}_{i,k}:n\in\Delta,i\in\mathcal{D} \}$ in \eqref{Frame:answers:lagrange}. After decoding of the desired symbols $\{\widetilde{w}^{(\theta)}_{i,k}\}_{i\in\mathcal{R}}$ for all $k\in[0:K)$, we can recover the partial file $\widetilde{\mathbf{W}}^{(\theta)}(\mathcal{R},:)$. 



In general, we obtain the following results regarding the \emph{feasible PIR coding framework} by Lemma \ref{theorem:tield size}.
\begin{Lemma}\label{lemma:feasible:PIR}
%There exist feasible PIR coded frameworks over any finite field $\mathbb{F}_q$ of size $N+\max\{K,\lambda\}$. %
The \emph{feasible PIR coding framework} can be implemented using either Lagrange codes or cross subspace alignment codes, and particularly these implemented feasible PIR coded frameworks can operate over any finite field $\mathbb{F}_q$ of size $q\geq N+\max\{K,\lambda\}$. 
\end{Lemma}

Although both implementations of the \emph{feasible PIR coding framework}s achieve the same performance in terms of finite field size, it is preferable to design an alternative framework with a smaller finite field size. %Additionally, different storage codes exhibit varying performances, such as the repair bandwidth when repairing failed servers. Therefore, it is also beneficial to design \emph{feasible PIR coding framework}s from based on the diverse performance characteristics of different storage coding systems. 
