\subsection{Flow past a cylinder }\label{FlowCylinder}
In the next example, we consider the benchmark problem of 2D unsteady flow around a cylinder with circular cross-section \cite{SchaferTurek96}, and we take the same setup for numerical simulations as in \cite{samujulia}. 
\begin{comment}
Following \cite{SchaferTurek96}, the computational domain is given by a rectangular channel with a circular hole (see Figure \ref{fig:mesh}):
\begin{equation}
    \Omega = \{(0,2.2) \times (0,0.41)\} \setminus \{x:(x-(0.2,0.2))^2 \leq 0.05^2\}.
\end{equation} 

\begin{figure}[ht!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{ImagesNSE/Mesh_1.jpeg}
    \caption{Computational grid of 2D flow past cylinder benchmark problem.}
    \label{fig:mesh}
\end{figure}

No slip boundary conditions are prescribed on the horizontal walls and on the cylinder, and a parabolic inflow profile is provided at the inlet
\begin{equation*}
    u(0,y,t) = (4U_my(A-y)/A^2,0)^T,
\end{equation*}
with $U_m = u(0,A/2,t) = 1.5\,m/s$ and $A = 0.41m$ the channel height. At the outlet boundary, we impose outflow boundary conditions, in order to test both ROMs detailed in Section \ref{ROMtimesplit}. The chosen Reynolds number is $Re = \dfrac{\overline{U}D}{\nu} = 100,$ expressed in term of the mean inflow velocity $\overline{U} = 1m/s,$ the cylinder diameter $D=0.1m,$ and the kinematic viscosity of the fluid $\nu = 10^{-3} m^2/s.$

For the evaluation of computational results, we are interested in studying the temporal evolution of the following quantities of interest:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $E_{Kin} = \dfrac12 \|u\|_{L^2}^2.$ (Kinetic Energy)
    \item $C_D = -\dfrac{2}{D\overline{U}^2}[(\partial_t u, v_D) + (u \cdot \nabla u,v_D) + \nu (\nabla u, \nabla v_D) - (p, \nabla \cdot v_D)].$ (Drag Coefficient)
\item $C_L = -\dfrac{2}{D\overline{U}^2}[(\partial_t u, v_L) + (u \cdot \nabla u,v_L) + \nu (\nabla u, \nabla v_L) - (p, \nabla \cdot v_L)].$ (Lift Coefficient)
\end{itemize}

for arbitrary test functions $v_D,v_L \in H^1$ such that $v_D = (1,0)^T$ on the boundary of the cylinder and vanishes on the other boundaries, $v_L = (0,1)^T$ on the boundary of the cylinder and vanishes on ther other boundaries. In the actual computations, we have used the approach described in \cite{DragLiftVolume} to fix the test functions $\bm{v}_D, \bm{v}_L$ and evaluate the drag and lift coefficients $C_D,C_L$ as volume integrals \cite{samujulia}. This allows to reduce the boundary approximation influences.
\end{comment}

